.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
SFTP version 3 (draft\-ietf\-secsh\-filexfer\-02), the version every implementation speaks, as client and server state machines over the byte stream of a channel running the "sftp" subsystem.
.PP
The file system is not here.  A server hands each request to the application as a parsed structure and the application answers it, now or later, with the response functions; a client turns calls into requests and delivers each response, with the tag its request carried, to a callback. Both ends frame the stream themselves: gf_sftp_server_input and gf_sftp_client_input take bytes as they come, in any chunking, and the write callback gets the bytes to send.  The write callback may take less than it is offered (a channel with a full window does): the rest is kept, up to a limit, and sent when gf_sftp_*_writable is called.
.PP
.nf
.RS
What the library enforces:
  \- the first packet is INIT, version 3 is what is spoken, and a peer
    that cannot speak it is refused;
  \- packets are at most max_packet bytes (default 256 KiB), checked when
    the length is readable, before the body arrives;
  \- a client's request ids are its own and each response must name one
    that is outstanding and be a response that kind of request can have
    (a READ is answered with DATA or STATUS, never NAME), and DATA is no
    longer than was asked for;
  \- a server answers each request once, with a response that fits it;
    an id it does not know, or one answered already, is an error of the
    application;
  \- handles are at most 256 bytes; paths and names cannot hold a NUL;
    counts are bounded by the packet that carries them;
  \- a client refuses a directory listing with an empty name, or a name
    with a slash in it, which could only be an attempt to have a file
    written outside the directory being copied.
A malformed request that can be named by its id is answered with
SSH_FX_BAD_MESSAGE and the session goes on; one that cannot, or a
protocol violation by the peer, ends the session (the disconnect event).
.RE
.fi
.PP
Extensions (OpenSSH's PROTOCOL, the ones its sftp client and server use: posix\-rename, statvfs, fstatvfs, hardlink, fsync, lsetstat, limits, expand\-path, copy\-data and users\-groups\-by\-id) are typed: a server lists the ones it implements in its configuration, which become the extension pairs of its VERSION, and decodes a request with gf_sftp_request_extension (a request of a known extension that is malformed is answered SSH_FX_BAD_MESSAGE by the library, so the application sees well\-formed ones; one it did not advertise it should answer SSH_FX_OP_UNSUPPORTED); a client learns what the server advertised and its typed requests are refused (GF_ERR_UNSUPPORTED) for an extension the server did not, as OpenSSH's client does.  The replies of the extensions are checked as strictly as the replies of the base requests: an answer of the wrong kind or shape ends the session.  Other extensions pass through as before (gf_sftp_client_extended, and the ext_name and ext_data of a request).
.PP
Path handling is the application's, and it is where the security of an SFTP server is: nothing here confines a path to a directory.
